#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a7899e04-8be9-597a-a9b6-111201ebad2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8607" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ on the line segment $\\overline {AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x, y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a =  \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ (9-{2}^{2}-{1}^{2})-(9-{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,-2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =-4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{1-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1,c_2\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $4c_1 + 2c_2 = 5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+2(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ 5{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $c_2 = \\dfrac{c_1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $\\left(1, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2b224972-6a43-5691-9e46-abff577311d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8608" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 7-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1,-2z⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =4{c}_{1}+3+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{3}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t , \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t , \\quad c_2 = 3t , \\quad c_3 = 1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2t)+2(1-t) & =5 \\\\ 8t+2-2t & =5 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1,$ $c_2,$ and $c_3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0368b61d-2d85-58af-9af0-ec09edf877b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8693" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c?$\n\nI. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n\nII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n\nIII. $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨4,2⟩-⟨5,3⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nf_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) =-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191e0857-4af3-54f7-b915-63c472d131e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447488} {:answer/id #uuid "d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447578} {:answer/id #uuid "d41cdbde-f333-5a13-81de-47a16ab4d8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447579} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1348cc-1582-58d7-82fc-35147b2e1d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447592} {:answer/id #uuid "fac1e092-e375-5c3b-aaff-d23ceaeabfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d20d4356-f0e9-5813-aee5-0e7286bae160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447578} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea6eed03-9831-52dd-806d-2cce8dda7014" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04ad605f-8ccb-55ea-8b18-2d7ae6e4a34a" :question/math-academy-id "q-105744" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨3,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,4⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=4$ and $f(b)=-2,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ -2-4 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4⟩-⟨3,-1⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+5{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016736eb-caa6-5d48-8fbf-47af4434c9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447478} {:answer/id #uuid "0afea1a8-95a6-556d-a3e7-0e440998ec23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{f}_{x}(c)-4{f}_{y}(c)-3{f}_{z}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447485} {:answer/id #uuid "20aa54b1-4024-5833-be7a-094831fe92d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)+{f}_{z}(c)=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447492} {:answer/id #uuid "246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447494} {:answer/id #uuid "a04de735-f8d9-5805-bcc1-3800999914a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)-3{f}_{y}(c)-2{f}_{z}(c)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246ed905-e97b-5fe0-ab91-95d7dda0c05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447494} :answer-field/id #uuid "97502e7b-e0c1-5dff-b994-54b47731e1e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c15a8036-6858-5a29-a56c-8e96a5ccf287" :question/math-academy-id "q-130836" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-1,-1,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,0,-2⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=5$ and $f(b)=-1,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ -1-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,0,-2⟩-⟨-1,-1,3⟩) \\\\ -6 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,1,-5⟩ \\\\ -6 & =4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)-5{f}_{z}(c)=-6.$ *"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0222ab38-0a5d-5644-b548-b49f314a6f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447480} {:answer/id #uuid "6f049334-5ef6-5847-8455-946eb960be10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447534} {:answer/id #uuid "9718bb12-ffd6-5092-abb0-5cf104f4bf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{8}{3},\\frac{11}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447560} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447567} {:answer/id #uuid "df2c5b96-7e46-538a-b432-978f7151418c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{19}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a5f9b0-a9d6-5235-859a-a70242d8865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447567} :answer-field/id #uuid "301da0d6-3394-5ca9-822c-794b43878a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde771f2-0ae1-5186-919d-63d0f22b095d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36826" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}+xy+2{x}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,4)-f(0,3) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨3,4⟩-⟨0,3⟩) \\\\ 3(4)^{2}+3(4)+2(3)^{2}-(3(3)^{2}+0(3)+2(0)^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4x+y,x+6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}51 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =⟨4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ 51 & =12{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\ 51 & =13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-3}{4-3} & =\\frac{x-0}{3-0} \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $13{c}_{1}+9{c}_{2}=51,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{c}_{1}+9(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3) & =51 \\\\ 13{c}_{1}+3{c}_{1}+27 & =51 \\\\ 16{c}_{1} & =24 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7a5f5b-69d3-59c5-9fe5-d1c3092257a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484837} {:answer/id #uuid "1838f568-f154-5028-8a40-70828f862bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484865} {:answer/id #uuid "31c22ae3-b2f9-5039-88b3-e917ad61579f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484919} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f935b5-6dfe-5bcd-8f1a-c9dd6711bf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485207} {:answer/id #uuid "d9857530-796f-58fe-9f92-b5bb11123e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{11}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1838f568-f154-5028-8a40-70828f862bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484865} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf7b736-691e-5d1f-a95e-1aa8428199b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c8dce9-3855-5452-ba32-6512e799f65b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36836" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3)-f(0,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨0,2⟩) \\\\ (5(3)^{2}-2(2))-(5(2)^{2}-2(0)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2,10y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =-4+10{c}_{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3-2} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2{c}_{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-4=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,\\frac{5}{2}).$"}]}
